      "narrator": "en-US-AriaNeural"
    },
    "sourceLabels": [],
    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 0,
        "sourceEnd": 579,
        "text": "Good morning, everyone. Today I want to contrast two kinds of incentives: short-term rewards and long-term commitments. Let me start with a simple example. Imagine a company offers a bonus for each sale made this month. That is a short-term incentive. It produces quick action. But it may also produce rushed work and unhappy customers. Now imagine the same company offers a yearly profit share. That is a long-term incentive. It encourages careful decisions and patient relationship building. Neither is automatically better. The right choice depends on the task and the people.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "49dad067e7ea47ae6d5aacc2b3993d7dc7f9506cc0bfda754b34a225f245871e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 581,
        "sourceEnd": 990,
        "text": "Let me give you a second example from education. A student who receives a small prize for finishing homework tonight may study for an hour. A student who receives a scholarship after two years of steady effort may study for many months. The first reward is immediate; the second is delayed. Immediate rewards are powerful, but they fade quickly. Delayed rewards are weaker at first, but they can shape habits.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "60e435eab6e0d5f8fb9664e6374affb44ffe550edb44fd909b491ad6abab6ec3",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 992,
        "sourceEnd": 1427,
        "text": "Now, a common mistake is to assume that short-term incentives always harm long-term goals. That is not true. If the short-term reward is designed well, it can support the long-term goal. For example, a weekly team check-in can be a short-term routine that strengthens a long-term project. The key is alignment. When the short-term action moves you toward the long-term aim, the two work together. When it pulls you away, they conflict.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "94fc878c72c8d7a6df9ce133ef5a14838818db036e510c884dfbfc10fcd39bd8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1429,
        "sourceEnd": 1807,
        "text": "Another point: people often underestimate how much context matters. In a fast-changing market, short-term incentives may be necessary because long-term plans become outdated. In a stable environment, long-term incentives may be more effective because they reward consistency. So we should not ask, “Which incentive is best?” We should ask, “Which incentive fits this situation?”",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "729cd1b44b0d73fc5b81ff7c3fba04ee6a4ad3023b9e863986f7553f3b482ea5",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1809,
        "sourceEnd": 2270,
        "text": "Let me add a caution about measurement. If you measure only short-term output, people may ignore long-term quality. If you measure only long-term results, people may lose motivation during the long wait. A balanced system uses both. For instance, a sales team might receive a monthly bonus for customer satisfaction and a yearly bonus for repeat business. The monthly bonus is short-term; the yearly bonus is long-term. Together they cover both speed and trust.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "51212d254d8745c58a60b0db730aff7096df3bd848ee2584d126c50e77ec33d2",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 2272,
        "sourceEnd": 2804,
        "text": "What about unintended effects? Suppose a manager promises a reward for every complaint resolved within one day. Employees may close cases quickly without solving the real problem. The short-term incentive works, but the long-term outcome gets worse. This is why designers must predict how people will respond. If the reward is too narrow, people will optimize the narrow measure. If the reward is broad, people may not know what to do. The design challenge is to be specific enough to guide action, but broad enough to avoid gaming.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "96a45d3c64f58211116aa6d92d52d90b7c5c10201dd9c5119ee4cda8b0d6816d",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 2806,
        "sourceEnd": 3178,
        "text": "Finally, consider fairness. Even a well-designed incentive can fail if people believe it is unfair. If one team receives a large bonus and another team receives nothing for similar work, trust may drop. Trust is a long-term asset. Short-term savings from unequal rewards can cost long-term cooperation. Therefore, communicate the rules clearly and apply them consistently.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "94e2f935e0af0cc80f012c8a0a1a7a5dfde738e031e7089d2f4ed47bf1020bdc",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 3180,
        "sourceEnd": 3484,
        "text": "To summarize: short-term incentives create energy; long-term incentives create direction. Good systems use short-term rewards to start motion and long-term rewards to sustain it. The best design aligns the two, measures both, predicts responses, and protects fairness. Thank you. I’ll take questions now.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "f4eafc7f761ce57d90fad6f1ab8d89d4b317456b73c97964b0507dc589c8b4de",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      }
    ]
  },
